+          <li><svg class="ico"><use href="#i-x"/></svg><span><b>Nobody knows how to say it yet.</b> Age-eight in English, agé-opt in the Bucharest hybrid, or the intended age-infinit. A name people hesitate to pronounce loses word of mouth. The fix is to teach one spoken form from day one and never waver.</span></li>
+          <li><svg class="ico"><use href="#i-x"/></svg><span><b>Said fast in English, it brushes against aged.</b> For a longevity brand that is an ironic place to trip. Small risk, worth hearing out loud in the room before dismissing.</span></li>
+          <li><svg class="ico"><use href="#i-x"/></svg><span><b>Age is the heaviest word in the category.</b> It drags anti-ageing clinics and serums behind it. The 8 has to flip that reading instantly, every time, or the name lands in exactly the register the strategy avoids.</span></li>
+          <li><svg class="ico"><use href="#i-x"/></svg><span><b>Alphanumerics read biotech.</b> Age8 could be a Silicon Valley longevity startup. The Everyman warmth, the forty per cent that keeps this a club and not a protocol, has to come from everything around the name.</span></li>
